{"id":"qzpyq-chemistry-general-organic-chemistry-general-organic-chemistry-test-9","_quizrrTestId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47","title":"General Organic Chemistry - Test 9","questions":[{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253bbf","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253bbf","q":"<p>The&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>IUPAC</mi></math>&nbsp;name for the following compound is :</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-647dcae3ca3dc3f5.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 211px; height: 95px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-647dcae3ca3dc3f5.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-647dcae3ca3dc3f5.png\"> </p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></math>-dimenthyl-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>-carboxy-hex-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>-enal","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></math>-dimenthyl-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>-carboxy-hex-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>-enal","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></math>-dimenthyl-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>-carboxy-hex-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>-enoic acid","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>-formyl-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>-methy&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>-hex-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>-enoic acid"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-1db451bc5d4f3a02.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 166px; height: 118px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1db451bc5d4f3a02.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1db451bc5d4f3a02.png\"></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>5</mn></math>-dimenthyl-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>-carboxy-hex-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>-enoic acid</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253bc4","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253bc4","q":"<p>Match List I with List II<br /><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/37941d6f-2764-445f-88b8-73ed903bb395.png\" style=\"width: 550px; height: 285px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-4fd3791fd264720a.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-4fd3791fd264720a.png\"><br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:</p>","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>logK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub></math>, here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub></math> acid dissociation constant. More the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub></math> value, stronger is the acidic nature of the respective acid.</p><p>Hence, the lower <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub></math> value denotes a more powerful acid.  Phenols are more acidic than alcohols. Hence, ethanol has more <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub></math> value. Nitro group is the electron withdrawing group, hence it increases the acidic nature of phenols.  Among the given phenols para nitrophenol  is stronger acid. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub></math> values are matched below.</p><p>Ethanol <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>→</mo></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>9</mn></math></p><p>Phenol<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>→</mo><mn>10</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>-</mo></math>Nitrophenol <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>→</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mo>-</mo></math>Nitrophenol<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>→</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>Hence, the answer is option D.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253bc9","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253bc9","q":"<p>Given below are two statements:</p><p>Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>: A mixture of chloroform and aniline can be separated by simple distillation.</p><p>Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math>: When separating aniline from a mixture of aniline and water by steam distillation aniline boils below its boiling point.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:</p>","options":["Both Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>&nbsp;and Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math>&nbsp;are true","Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>&nbsp;is true but Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math>&nbsp;is false","Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>&nbsp;is false but Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math>&nbsp;is true","Both Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>&nbsp;and Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi></math>&nbsp;are false"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;of chloroform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>334</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;of aniline&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>457</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math></p><p>thus can be seprated of simple distillation.</p><p>Statement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;: Mixture of aniline and water seprated by simple distillation.</p><p>A suitable method for separating a mixture of aniline and chloro form would be steam distillation. Steam distillation is the process used to separate aromatic compound from a mixture because of their temperature sensitivity. Therefore, steam distillation is an ideal method for their separation</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253bce","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253bce","q":"The correct stability order of the following species/molecules is:\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-e76460bf507e5b21.png?alt=media&v=stem2\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e76460bf507e5b21.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e76460bf507e5b21.png\"><br/>","options":["$q\\gt r\\gt p$","$r\\gt q\\gt p$","$q\\gt p\\gt r$","$\\mathrm{p}\\gt\\mathrm{q}\\gt\\mathrm{r}$"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"q is aromatic\nr is non-aromatic\np is antiaromatic\n$\\mathrm{q} \\gt \\mathrm{r} \\gt \\mathrm{p}$ (order of stability)\nAromatic > non-aromatic > antiaromatic","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253bd3","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253bd3","q":"The isomeric deuterated bromide with molecular formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mi>DBr</mi></math> having two chiral carbon atoms is","options":["2-Bromo-1-deuterobutane","2-Bromo-2-deuterobutane","2-Bromo-3-deuterobutane","2-Bromo-1-deutero-2-methylpropane"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>The isomeric deuterated bromide with molecular formula&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mi>DBr</mi></math>&nbsp;having two chiral carbon atoms is&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-e0a646c434c9bbdc.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 210px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e0a646c434c9bbdc.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e0a646c434c9bbdc.png\"></p><p>2-Bromo-3-deuterobutane</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253bd8","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253bd8","q":"<p><img height=\"76\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/88cdd033-263a-49b6-8f82-7f67d3a891b8-image.png\" width=\"408\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-07c8930fa1013b5f.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-07c8930fa1013b5f.png\"></p><p>The stable carbocation formed in the above reaction is :</p>","options":["<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/7c36fb91-1e56-4f74-b4be-75d4de12d334-9711836_A.png\" style=\"width: 120px; height: 40px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e2daa508299c3d91.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e2daa508299c3d91.png\"></p>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>&#8853;</mo></mover><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>&#8853;</mo></mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<p><img height=\"107\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/6a3f25c5-fb94-44b8-b127-ccaa093d4fe8-image.png\" width=\"119\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e4ce20c1de45f692.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-e4ce20c1de45f692.png\"></p><p>&nbsp;</p>"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>Benzene undergoes Friedel-Crafts Alkylation&nbsp;reaction with propylchloride in presence of anhydrous AlCl<sub>3&nbsp;</sub>to form isopropyl benzene.</p><p>Propyl carbocation formed undergoes rearrangement to form secondary propyl carbocation</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/a6ade6b8-0679-4216-95ae-16c48cc7439e-image.png\" style=\"width: 570px; height: 150px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c93a34a6429ebb37.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c93a34a6429ebb37.png\"></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253bdd","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253bdd","q":"In the following skew conformation of ethane, <math><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#39;</mi></mrow></msup><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>&#8243;</mo></mrow></msup></math> dihedral angle is:<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-892563373e772dbf.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-892563373e772dbf.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-892563373e772dbf.png\">","options":["<math><msup><mrow><mn>58</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math>","<math><msup><mrow><mn>120</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math>","<math><msup><mrow><mn>149</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math>","<math><msup><mrow><mn>151</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math>"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"The structure given in question is in staggered form. Hence, the two planes are <math><mn>120</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> apart.<br  />Given difference in angle of <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math> bond is <math><mn>29</mn><mo>.</mo></math><br  /><math><mo>&#8756;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mn>120</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>29</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>149</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253be2","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253be2","q":"The correct nomenclature for the following compound is :\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-2f81d620d8d295ef.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-2f81d620d8d295ef.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-2f81d620d8d295ef.png\"><br>","options":["2-formyl-4-hydroxyhept-7-enoic acid","2-formyl-4-hydroxyhept-6-enoic acid","2-carboxy-4-hydroxyhept-7-enal","2-carboxy-4-hydroxyhept-6-enal"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-33c0968dca637046.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-33c0968dca637046.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-33c0968dca637046.png\"><br  />2-formly-4-hydroxyhept-6-enoic acid","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253be7","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253be7","q":"Which purification technique is used for high boiling organic liquid compound (decomposes near its boiling point) ?","options":["Simple distillation","Steam distillation","Fractional distillation","Reduced pressure distillation"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"Reduced pressure distillation or vacuum distillation is used for the purification of high boiling organic liquids which decomposes at or below their boiling point.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253bec","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253bec","q":"Relative stability of the contributing structures is :\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-93b23b56ad2a1a2a.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-93b23b56ad2a1a2a.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-93b23b56ad2a1a2a.png\"><br>","options":["(I) \\(  \\gt  \\) (II) \\(  \\gt  \\) (III)","(I) \\(  \\gt  \\) (III) \\(  \\gt  \\) (II)","\\(\\text { (II) }  \\gt  \\text { (I) }  \\gt  \\text { (III) }\\)","\\(\\text { (III) }  \\gt  \\text { (II) }  \\gt  \\text { (I) }\\)"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"(1) Neutral structures are more stable than charged ones. Therefore I is more stable than II and III.\n(2) +ve charge on less electronegative atom is more stable i.e., $\\mathrm{C}^{\\oplus}$ is more stable than $\\mathrm{O}^{\\oplus}$ Order is I  >  III  >  III","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253bf1","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253bf1","q":"Which of the following is the correct IUPAC name of given organic compound $(\\mathrm{X})$ ?<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-cbd82ee26792f6a6.png?alt=media&v=stem2\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-cbd82ee26792f6a6.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-cbd82ee26792f6a6.png\"><br/>","options":["2-Bromo-2-methylbut-2-ene","3-Bromo-3-methylprop-2-ene","1-Bromo-2-methylbut-2-ene","4-Bromo-3-methylbut-2-ene"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-a2deb09e24b364ae.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-a2deb09e24b364ae.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-a2deb09e24b364ae.png\"><br  />1-Bromo-2-methyl but-2-ene","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253bf6","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253bf6","q":"<p>The correct order for acidity of the following hydroxyl compound is</p><p>(A)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>OH</mi></math></p><p>(B)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COH</mi></math></p><p>(C)&nbsp;<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-cd85418370093a04.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 150px; height: 77px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-cd85418370093a04.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-cd85418370093a04.png\"></p><p>(D)&nbsp;<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-5af2fe313eac6fde.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 74px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-5af2fe313eac6fde.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-5af2fe313eac6fde.png\"></p><p>(E)&nbsp;<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-bfe4817e1545b255.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 78px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-bfe4817e1545b255.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-bfe4817e1545b255.png\"></p><p>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:</p>","options":["C &gt; E &gt; D &gt; B &gt; A","E &gt; D &gt; C &gt; B &gt; A","D &gt; E &gt; C &gt; A &gt; B","E &gt; C &gt; D &gt; A &gt; B"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>The compounds A &amp; B are alcohols, and rest of the compounds are phenols. Phenols are more acidic than alcohols. Among the A &amp;B, methyl alcohol is more acidic than tertiary butyl alcohol as alkyl groups decreases the acidic nature by electron releasing tendency. Among the phenols, the phenol with electron withdrawing group is more acidic.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>(Electron withdrawing group) group will increase the acidity, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>OCH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>(Electron releasing&nbsp;group) group at para position will decrease the acidity.</p><p>So, Acidic strength&nbsp;E&gt;C&gt;D&gt;A&gt;B</p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253bfb","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253bfb","q":"The correct match between items I and II is :\n\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-89fad7db683468ac.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-89fad7db683468ac.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-89fad7db683468ac.png\">","options":["$(\\mathrm{A}) \\rightarrow(\\mathrm{S}) ;(\\mathrm{B}) \\rightarrow(\\mathrm{R}) ;(\\mathrm{C}) \\rightarrow(\\mathrm{P})$","(A) $\\rightarrow(\\mathrm{Q}) ;(\\mathrm{B}) \\rightarrow(\\mathrm{R}) ;(\\mathrm{C}) \\rightarrow(\\mathrm{S})$","(A) \\rightarrow  (R); (B) \\rightarrow  (P); (C) \\rightarrow  (S)","(A) $\\rightarrow(\\mathrm{Q}) ;(\\mathrm{B}) \\rightarrow(\\mathrm{R}) ;(\\mathrm{C}) \\rightarrow(\\mathrm{P})$"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ : Sugar - Recrystallisation\r\n\r\n$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}:$ Aniline - Separation by steam distillation\r\n\r\n$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}:$ Toluene - Differential extraction","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253c00","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253c00","q":"Which of the following compounds does not exhibit resonance ?","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>OCH</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-382d9ce07a8aba47.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 124px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-382d9ce07a8aba47.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-382d9ce07a8aba47.png\">","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>CONH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>CH</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>CHCH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mi>CH</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>CH</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math><br  />No conjugation thus resonance is not possible.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253c05","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253c05","q":"The incorrect statements regarding geometrical isomerism are :\n(A) Propene shows geometrical isomerism.\n(B) Trans isomer has identical atoms/groups on the opposite sides of the double bond.\n(C) Cis-but-2-ene has higher dipole moment than trans-but-2-ene.\n(D) 2-methylbut-2-ene shows two geometrical isomers.\n(E) Trans-isomer has lower melting point than cis isomer.\n\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A) and (E) Only","(A), (D) and (E) Only","(B) and (C) Only","(C), (D) and (E) Only"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"(A) $\\mathrm{CH}_3-\\mathrm{CH}=\\mathrm{CH}_2$. GI is not possible\n(B) Trans isomer has identical atoms/groups on the opposite side of double bond.\n\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-768fd820a5ebe084.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-768fd820a5ebe084.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-768fd820a5ebe084.png\"><br>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253c0a","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253c0a","q":"Among the following marked proton of which compound shows lowest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>pK</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></msub></math> value?","options":["<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/dd28d7e4-cacd-4287-bde6-ff722a03f1f8-0d8420a0-34e7-40f1-9be6-a9896e227183-9757615_D.png\"><br>","<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/8dfb8a13-cb39-44a7-9332-a8c59101b9c8-58c68ea3-1ee9-4630-802e-4e14f86421c0-9757615_C.png\"><br>","<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/efee0a33-570a-461f-bb52-d8138c4ed8e4-83dc113a-4b06-4895-bfff-58007c5e0109-9757615_B.png\"><br>","<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/pyq/jee_main/bb91f14d-7d8a-465c-ac0f-5ad567a37dd1-57b5ea86-6b9d-4289-b2d5-f4d7a748e1f5-9757615_A.png\"><br>"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>The smaller the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pKa</mi></math>, the stronger the acid. The acidic nature of the compound depends on the stability of conjugate base.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-a210c2442b59b4e7.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 219px; height: 144px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-a210c2442b59b4e7.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-a210c2442b59b4e7.png\"></p><p>The stability of conjugate base formed by above compound is more stable than other three compounds given.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-0cd0da695ac405ff.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 328px; height: 114px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0cd0da695ac405ff.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0cd0da695ac405ff.png\"></p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-9b3d35fdc5cfa27e.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 360px; height: 125px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9b3d35fdc5cfa27e.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9b3d35fdc5cfa27e.png\"></p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-4bd961c405d8f65e.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 464px; height: 149px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-4bd961c405d8f65e.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-4bd961c405d8f65e.png\"></p><p>So it has least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pKa</mi></math> value.</p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47","_resolvedFrom":"marks_login_id","_marksId":"648b5a6d417cc3fb48d67c4a"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253c0f","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253c0f","q":"Which of the following conformations will be the most stable?","options":["<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-17374f2fa6766b68.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 221px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-17374f2fa6766b68.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-17374f2fa6766b68.png\">&nbsp;","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-d87ab9ed4880b58b.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 178px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-d87ab9ed4880b58b.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-d87ab9ed4880b58b.png\">&nbsp;","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-0e1cfef217d8cbf0.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 221px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0e1cfef217d8cbf0.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0e1cfef217d8cbf0.png\">&nbsp;","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-56194f503365aef2.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 175px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-56194f503365aef2.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-56194f503365aef2.png\">&nbsp;"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>Lithium borohydride is commonly used for the selective reduction of esters and lactones to the corresponding alcohols in the presence of carboxylic acids, tertiary amides, and nitriles.</p><p></p><p>&nbsp;</p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253c14","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253c14","q":"The molecular formula of second homologue in the homologous series of mono carboxylic acids is _________.","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>The general molecular formula for carboxylic acid is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>COOH</mi></math>.</p><p>In organic chemistry, a homologous series is a sequence of compounds with the same functional group and similar chemical properties in which the members of the series can be branched or unbranched, or differ by molecular formula of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and molecular mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>14</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u</mi></math>.</p><p>Second homologue : <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi></math><br/><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>Hence, the answer is B.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253c19","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253c19","q":"Match List-I with List-II<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-74322243fbb6bc20.png?alt=media&v=stem2\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-74322243fbb6bc20.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-74322243fbb6bc20.png\"><br />Choose the correct answer from the options given below :","options":["(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)","(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_main%252FtON_a59caiSjXxepBxtTO0V3O4q55xXaFlrID0Q2RPU.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FtON_a59caiSjXxepBxtTO0V3O4q55xXaFlrID0Q2RPU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2FtON_a59caiSjXxepBxtTO0V3O4q55xXaFlrID0Q2RPU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47","_marksId":"66446748970e6e9ab542d1e8"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253c1e","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253c1e","q":"<p>The difference in energy between the actual structure and the lowest energy resonance structure for the given compound is:</p>","options":["electromeric energy","resonance energy","ionization energy","hyperconjugation energy"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>The difference in energy between the actual structure and the lowest energy resonance structure for the given compound is known as resonance energy.</p><p>The resonance energy of a compound is a measure of the extra stability of the conjugated system compared to the corresponding number of isolated double bonds.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253d04","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253d04","q":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of an organic compound containing chlorine gave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of silver chloride in Carius estimation. The percentage of chlorine present in the compound is [in nearest integer]<br />(Given: Molar mass of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ag</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>108</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mol</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> and that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cl</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>35</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>)","options":["","","",""],"answer":"40","answers":["40"],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"Percentage of chlorine in the organic compound=<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>atomic</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>chlorine</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>AgCl</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>formed</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>molecular</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mass</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>ofAgCl</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>weight</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>organic</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>compound</mi></mrow></mfrac></math><p>Mass of organic compound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math></p><p>Mass of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>AgCl</mi></math>&nbsp;formed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math></p><p>Mass of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cl</mi></math>&nbsp;in organic compound&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>35</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>143</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>Percentage of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cl</mi></math>&nbsp;in the compound&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>35</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>143</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>39</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>%</mo></math></p><p>Nearest integer=40.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47","correctValue":"40","_resolvedFrom":"official_bank_unique"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253d09","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253d09","q":"The number of optical isomers in following compound is: ________\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-3726f134bf3cd5bc.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-3726f134bf3cd5bc.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-3726f134bf3cd5bc.png\"><br>","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-1335bb13c84dcc1b.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1335bb13c84dcc1b.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1335bb13c84dcc1b.png\"><br  />Total chiral centre $=5$\nNo. of optical isomers $=2^5=32$.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253d0e","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253d0e","q":"<p>In Duma&#39;s method of estimation of nitrogen, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1840</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of an organic compound gave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi></math> of nitrogen collected at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>287</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>758</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Hg</mi></math>&nbsp;pressure. The percentage composition of nitrogen in the compound is ______.&nbsp;(Round off to the Nearest Integer).</p><p>[Given : Aqueous tension at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>287</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>&nbsp;of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Hg</mi></math>]</p>","options":["","","",""],"answer":"0","answers":["0"],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>In Duma&#39;s method of estimation of Nitrogen. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1840</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>gm</mi></math> of organic compound gave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi></math> of nitrogen which is collected at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>287</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>758</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Hg</mi></math>.</p><p>Given ;&nbsp;</p><p>Aqueous tension at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>287</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Hg</mi></math>.</p><p>Hence, actual pressure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>758</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>744</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>&nbsp;of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Hg</mi></math>.</p><p>Volume of nitrogen at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>STP</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>273</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>744</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>287</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>760</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>27</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>935</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8757;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>22400</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> at STP weighs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>28</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>gm</mi></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>27</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>94</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mL</mi></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>STP</mi></math> weighs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>28</mn><mn>22400</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>27</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>94</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>gm</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0349</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>gm</mi></math></p><p>Hence&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math> of Nitrogen&nbsp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0349</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1840</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>97</mn><mo>%</mo></math></p><p>Round off. Answer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>19</mn><mo>%</mo></math></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47","correctValue":0,"_resolvedFrom":"official_bank_unique"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253d13","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253d13","q":"Total number of optically active compounds from the following is ______\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-0f58746216829830.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0f58746216829830.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0f58746216829830.png\"><br>","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"},{"id":"qz_69de4f392ee0e063d9253d18","_quizrrId":"69de4f392ee0e063d9253d18","q":"In the sulphur estimation, 0.20 g of a pure organic compound gave 0.40 g of barium sulphate. The percentage of sulphur in the compound is ______ $\\times 10^{-1} \\%$.\n(Molar mass : $\\mathrm{O}=16, \\mathrm{~S}=32, \\mathrm{Ba}=137$ in $\\mathrm{g} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ )","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quizrr PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"$\\begin{array}{ll}\\text { Organic Compound } \\longrightarrow & \\mathrm{BaSO}_4 \\\\ 0.20 \\mathrm{gm} & 0.40 \\mathrm{gm} \\\\ & \\frac{0.40}{233} \\mathrm{~mol}\\left(\\mathrm{BaSO}_4\\right) \\\\ & \\frac{0.40}{233} \\mathrm{~mol} \\text { (Sulphur) } \\\\ & \\frac{0.40}{233} \\times 32 \\mathrm{gm} \\text { (sulphur) }\\end{array}\n\n$\\% \\mathrm{~S}=\\frac{\\frac{0.40 \\times 32}{233} \\times 100}{0.20}=27.5 \\%$ or $275 \\times 10^{-1} \\%","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f47e39d99b57bc5b47"}]}